WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.900
In 1978, a then-unknown Nick Castle first
gave shape to Michael Myers in John Carpenter's

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:07.720
iconic horror flick Halloween.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:11.920
Though Castle didn't reprise his role in any
of the Halloween sequels or reboots from 1981

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:17.110
to 2009, the horror world was rocked when
he donned the mask and coveralls once again

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:20.440
for the 2018 sequel, also titled Halloween.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:23.380
Here's how Castle became Halloween's Michael
Myers, a.k.a.

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:24.320
"The Shape."

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:29.980
Castle explained during an October 2018 appearance
on the podcast The Subplot that he sort of

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.020
stumbled into playing Michael Myers.

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:35.620
He was friends with John Carpenter for some
time before the young director set out to

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:40.319
bring Halloween to the big screen, even appearing
in an uncredited cameo in Carpenter's debut

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:41.660
film Dark Star.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:45.700
When Carpenter went into production on Halloween,
Castle asked his buddy if he could hang around

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:50.019
set and watch him work, hoping the experience
would "demystify" what directing is really

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:53.020
like, since Castle had aspirations to direct
himself.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:56.520
Rather than just have his pal hang about,
though, Carpenter asked him to stand in for

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:57.520
the Myers role.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:01.959
"He went yeah, why don't you be the guy, then,
and put on the mask if you're here and around

00:01:01.959 --> 00:01:02.959
anyhow?

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:04.060
He went, 'Okay.'"

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:08.720
As for how Carpenter got his non-actor buddy
into the menacing mind of Michael Myers, Castle

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:12.620
noted that there wasn't much direction other
than "walk there," or "tilt your head."

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:17.220
"I basically asked the actor question, 'What
is my motivation?'

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:22.100
And he said, 'Your motivation is to walk from
this point to the endpoint.'"

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.640
That "tilt your head" moment has since become
a fan-favorite part of Carpenter's original,

00:01:26.649 --> 00:01:30.850
so much so that Halloween reboot director
David Gordon Green called back to it in the

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:32.100
2018 movie.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:36.240
Carpenter himself has since clarified his
approach to casting Castle in the role and

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:40.600
helping him become Michael Myers, noting his
pal's peculiar stride as a key element in

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:41.939
bringing the baddie to life.

00:01:41.939 --> 00:01:44.420
He told Entertainment Weekly on October 2018,

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:48.460
"We were buddies in film school, and I knew
him, and he just has a grace to him.

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:50.000
And his dad was a choreographer.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.259
I don't know if he learned anything from his
father, but he moved in a way that nobody

00:01:53.259 --> 00:01:54.399
else moves like."

00:01:54.399 --> 00:01:55.740
Castle chimed in, saying,

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:58.400
"What I really did was listen to what John
told me to do.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.400
He was really very much puppeteering.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:04.140
It was John telling me, 'Go fast,' 'Go slow,'
'Tilt your head,' and puppeteering me through

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:05.140
the thing."

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:10.320
In 2017, a video surfaced on Twitter that
reportedly showed Castle wearing a Michael

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:12.620
Myers mask while walking on a treadmill.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:16.820
There's little question that Castle's gait
is a big part of what makes Michael Myers,

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:20.580
so it makes sense that most of the actor's
pre-production prep work involved ambling

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:21.720
across a treadmill.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:26.200
Walking in a brooding-yet-purposeful fashion
is sort of Myers' thing, outside of offing

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:29.680
unsuspecting teens and trying to get that
butcher's knife into Laurie Strode's back,

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:30.540
of course.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:34.800
Even so, savvy Halloween-heads are often quick
to point out the difference in that walk throughout

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.940
the Halloween sequels, with some even noticing
the difference in Green's latest chapter.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:43.460
As we've come to find out, actor James Jude
Courtney did the vast majority of the walking

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:47.950
and stalking in the 2018 movie, with the head
tilt scene in Green's Halloween marking the

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:51.380
only moment in the film where Castle is actually
playing the part.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:57.040
In September 2019, Castle posted another clip
of himself creepily walking on his home treadmill

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.460
with his Michael Myers mask covering his face.

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:03.680
The video was meant to tease that he's preparing
to become the Shape again for 2020's Halloween

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:06.150
Kills and 2021's Halloween Ends.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:10.230
Both Castle and Courtney are scheduled to
appear as Michael Myers in those films, and

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:13.960
the second of the two sequels is said to put
an official end to the Michael Myers/Laurie

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:15.180
Strode storyline.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:19.220
Jamie Lee Curtis is seemingly retiring the
role that made her a scream queen.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:23.540
"Happy Halloween, Michael."

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:28.840
For Castle's part, it appears he's taking
to heart what may be his final turns behind

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:29.980
the eerie mask.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:35.420
If the hilarious Twitter post is any indication,
the actor is doing his best to get into, ahem,

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:38.540
shape, and maybe doing a bit more walking
this go-around.

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:43.720
Castle will return to action when Halloween
Kills makes its premiere on October 16, 2020.

